# How do we add up everyone's welfare?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Utilitarian, Rawlsian, or something else: what is a social welfare function? Why does the answer change which policy is best?"
next: {"title":"Is the market fair?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/29-is-the-market-fair.cast"}

## Three plans, two rules
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - 'A: Big dock'
      - 'B: Small dock + aid'
      - 'C: Fishing fund'
    series:
      - {name: Boatyard families, values: [9, 7, 5]}
      - {name: Fishers, values: [3, 4.5, 5]}
    value_labels: true
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 10
    y_label: Wellbeing (illustrative)
    label_size: 26
    box:
      x: 80
      'y': 150
      w: 610
      h: 490

    scratch sum work ["Add up:","A  9 + 3 = 12","B  7 + 4.5 = 11.5","C  5 + 5 = 10","→ A wins"] x 850 y 280 font_size 30 hidden true
    scratch mins work ["Worst-off:","A  3","B  4.5","C  5","→ C wins"] x 850 y 300 font_size 30 hidden true
    portrait rawls x 850 y 420 width 170 of "John Rawls" cameo true hidden true
    text swf "social welfare\nfunction" #8a5fa8 x 850 y 330 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "Three plans, two rules"

When a policy helps some people and hurts others, how do we decide whether society as a whole is better off?
    draw axes

To see why it's hard, take Harbourtown's council, choosing between (@draw legend@) three plans. Say we could score each family's wellbeing ou (@highlight axes@)t of ten; these numbers are illustrative.

B: Plan A bu (@draw bar_1@)ilds a big new dock. The boatyard families do great, nine. The fishers get three.

B: Plan B  (@draw bar_2@)is a smaller dock plus fuel aid for the fishers: seven and four and a half. Pl (@draw bar_3@)an C puts it all in a fishing fund: five each.

    quiz "Which plan would you pick for Harbourtown?" judge false
        right: Hold on to that answer: which one wins depends on a rule you may not know you're using.
        * A: Big dock
        * B: Small dock + aid
        * C: Fishing fund

Rule  (@draw sum_box sum_line_1@)one: add everyone's wellbeing u (@draw sum_line_2@)p. A gives twe (@draw sum_line_3@)lve, B eleven and  (@draw sum_line_4@)a half, C ten. A w (@draw sum_line_5@)ins.

That's the utilitarian rule of Jeremy  (@highlight sum_line_5 effect underline@)Bentham: the greatest happiness, added up across people.

B: So a utilitarian doesn't care who gets the points?
    highlight bar_1

Not with these numbers. With money they would: a hundred dollars adds more wellbeing to a fisher than to a boatyard owner. But these scores are wellbeing already, so a point is a point.
    highlight sum_line_2 sum_line_3 sum_line_4

    erase sum

B: Rule t (@draw mins_box mins_line_1@)wo looks only at whoever is worst off. That's the fishers (@draw mins_line_2@): three under A, fo (@draw mins_line_3@)ur and a half under B, fi (@draw mins_line_4@)ve under C. C w (@draw mins_line_5@)ins.

    erase mins

B: Economists call that the Rawlsian rule, after th (@draw rawls@)e philosopher John Rawls. He meant it for the basic rules of a society, n (@highlight bar_3 effect box@)ot happiness scores, but the name stuck.

    erase rawls

Same numbers,  (@highlight bar_1 bar_3 effect box@)two rules, two winners. A rule that turns everyone's wellbeing into one score for society has a name (@draw swf@): a social welfare function.

    pause 0.5

## A dial between them
vars:
    lam:
      value: 1
      min: 0
      max: 6
      step: 0.05
    WA:
      expr: 9+3*lam
    WB:
      expr: 7+4.5*lam
    WC:
      expr: 5+5*lam
    Wm:
      expr: max(WA,max(WB,WC))
    iA:
      expr: floor(WA/Wm+0.0001)
    iB:
      expr: floor(WB/Wm+0.0001)
    iC:
      expr: floor(WC/Wm+0.0001)
    bx:
      expr: (170*iA+340*iB+510*iC)/(iA+iB+iC)

    shape glow #f2c14e shape rect x 170 y 262 width 165 height 164 bind.height 12*Wm+24 bind.x (150*iA+330*iB+510*iC)/(iA+iB+iC) bind.y 215+12*Wm/2+12 style.fill #f2c14e style.opacity 0.35 style.stroke_width 0 hidden true
    path base thick #3d3833 points [[60,215],[600,215]] hidden true
    shape barA #2f6b8f shape rect x 150 y 252 width 130 height 144 bind.height 12*WA bind.y 215+12*WA/2 style.fill #2f6b8f style.opacity 0.85 hidden true
    text valA "{WA:1}" x 150 y 352 font_size 30 bind.y 215+12*WA+28 hidden true
    text labA "A\nBig dock" x 150 y 175 font_size 24 hidden true
    shape barB #2f6b8f shape rect x 330 y 249 width 130 height 138 bind.height 12*WB bind.y 215+12*WB/2 style.fill #2f6b8f style.opacity 0.85 hidden true
    text valB "{WB:1}" x 330 y 346 font_size 30 bind.y 215+12*WB+28 hidden true
    text labB "B\nSmall dock + aid" x 330 y 175 font_size 24 hidden true
    shape barC #2f6b8f shape rect x 510 y 240 width 130 height 120 bind.height 12*WC bind.y 215+12*WC/2 style.fill #2f6b8f style.opacity 0.85 hidden true
    text valC "{WC:1}" x 510 y 328 font_size 30 bind.y 215+12*WC+28 hidden true
    text labC "C\nFishing fund" x 510 y 175 font_size 24 hidden true
    group bars members ["base","barA","valA","labA","barB","valB","labB","barC","valC","labC"] hidden true
    math formula tex "W = U_{yard} + {lam}\\,U_{fish}" size 40 colors {"U_{fish}":"#2f6b8f"} x 815 y 560 hidden true
    text util "weight 1: utilitarian" #8f887c x 815 y 480 font_size 30 hidden true
    text rawls "weight → ∞: Rawls" #b5482e x 815 y 430 font_size 30 hidden true
    text prio "in between:\nprioritarian" #8a5fa8 x 815 y 340 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "A dial between them"

B: Those tw (@draw bars formula@)o rules look like opposites. They're really the two ends of one dial.

B: Give each fisher point a weight. At weight one it's plain adding up, and A (@draw glow util@) wins with twelve.
    highlight formula

And if I turn the weight up? My guess is  (@highlight barC@)C jumps straight to the top, since it's best for the fishers.

    quiz "At weight two, which plan wins?" judge false voice b
        right: Watch the bars as the weight doubles.
        * A: Big dock
        * B: Small dock + aid
        * C: Fishing fund

B: B wins: sixteen, against fifteen for A and for C.
    animate lam 2 duration 3

    erase util

Not C? But C is the fishers' best plan.
    highlight barC valC

B: Under C (@highlight barC@) the boatyard drops to five, and that loss still counts (@animate lam 5 duration 3@). Turn it up to five, and now C wins: thirty against twenty-nine and a half.

And all the way up?
    animate lam 6 duration 2

B: Then only the worst-off count. That's (@draw rawls@) Rawls: the dial turned to infinity.

So the something else is anywhere in between: extra weight on whoever i (@draw prio@)s worse off, but not all of it. Philosophers call that prioritarianism.

    animate lam 1 duration 2

B: Try it yourself: drag the weight and find the point where the big dock stops winning.
    explore params ["lam"]

    animate lam 1.4 duration 2

It's at one and a third. Count a fisher's point a third more than a boatyard point, and plan A loses its lead.
    highlight barA barB

    pause 0.5

## Who measured the points?
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - 'A: Big dock'
      - 'B: Small dock + aid'
      - 'C: Fishing fund'
    series:
      - {name: Boatyard families, values: [[9, 7, 5], [9, 7, 5]]}
      - {name: Fishers, values: [[3, 4.5, 5], [6, 9, 10]]}
    stage: 0
    value_labels: true
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 14
    y_label: Wellbeing (illustrative)
    label_size: 26
    box:
      x: 80
      'y': 150
      w: 600
      h: 490
sources:
    - id: robbins
      title: An Essay on the Nature and Significance of Economic Science
      authors: Lionel Robbins
      year: 1932
      finding: Argues that interpersonal comparisons of satisfaction cannot be made on scientific grounds; they are value judgements.
    - id: arrow
      title: Social Choice and Individual Values
      authors: Kenneth J. Arrow
      year: 1951
      finding: 'Impossibility theorem: with only individual rankings (no interpersonal comparisons), no rule for aggregating them into a social ranking satisfies unrestricted domain, weak Pareto, independence of irrelevant alternatives and non-dictatorship.'
    - id: sen
      title: Collective Choice and Social Welfare
      authors: Amartya Sen
      year: 1970
      finding: Shows that allowing interpersonal comparisons of welfare opens the way to consistent social welfare functions, escaping Arrow's impossibility.
    - id: greenbook
      title: 'The Green Book: appraisal and evaluation in central government (2026)'
      authors: HM Treasury
      year: 2026
      finding: Welfare weight of a group = (national median equivalised income / group's median equivalised income)^1.25, 1.25 being the elasticity of marginal utility of consumption; a group at half the median income gets weight 2^1.25 ≈ 2.4.
      url: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-green-book--2/the-green-book-2026
    - id: atkinson
      title: On the measurement of inequality
      authors: Anthony B. Atkinson
      year: 1970
      finding: 'Social welfare W = Σ U^(1−ε)/(1−ε): ε = 0 is the plain sum; as ε grows the function puts more weight on the worst-off, approaching maximin.'
      doi: 10.1016/0022-0531(70)90039-6

    text x2 "fishers' scale × 2" #2f6b8f x 850 y 560 font_size 30 hidden true
    scratch sum work ["Add up:","A  9 + 6 = 15","B  7 + 9 = 16","C  5 + 10 = 15","→ B wins"] x 850 y 330 font_size 30 hidden true
    text robbins_t "Robbins, 1932:\nno scientific\ncomparison" cites ["robbins"] x 850 y 420 font_size 28 hidden true
    portrait arrow_p cites ["arrow"] x 860 y 520 width 160 of "Kenneth Arrow" cameo true hidden true
    text arrow_t "Arrow, 1951:\nimpossible" cites ["arrow"] x 860 y 300 font_size 28 hidden true
    text sen_t "Sen:\ncompare,\nand it's possible" cites ["sen"] x 850 y 420 font_size 28 hidden true
    text open "Which rule?\nopen" #b5482e x 850 y 500 font_size 36 hidden true
    text uk "UK Treasury:\n£1 at half the\nmedian income\ncounts ×2.4" cites ["greenbook"] x 850 y 300 font_size 28 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch m cites ["atkinson"] work [{"tex":"\\text{Utilitarian: } W = U_1 + U_2 + \\dots"},{"tex":"\\text{Rawls: } W = \\min(U_1, U_2, \\dots)"},{"tex":"\\text{Atkinson: } W = \\sum_i U_i^{1-\\varepsilon}/(1-\\varepsilon)"}] x 500 y 420 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Who measured the points?"

B: A weight on the worse-off does the job. But here's my problem: who measured a fisher's wellbeing in points?
    draw axes legend bar_1 bar_2 bar_3

Nobody can, directly. So suppose the fishers' (@draw x2@) scale simply runs twice as long: same lives, every number doubled.

    animate stage 1 duration 2.5 ghost true

B: Then plai (@draw sum_box sum_line_1 sum_line_2 sum_line_3 sum_line_4 sum_line_5@)n adding up picks B, not A. The neutral-looking sum was hiding a weight all along.

    erase sum x2

    animate stage 0 duration 1.5

In 1932 Lionel Robbin (@draw robbins_t@)s argued that comparing one person's satisfaction with another's isn't science at all.

    erase robbins_t

B: And in 1951 Kenneth Arrow  (@draw arrow_p arrow_t@)proved that with only each person's ranking of the options, no rule for combinin (@highlight arrow_t@)g them meets a few reasonable conditions at once, short of a dictator.

    erase arrow_p arrow_t

Amartya Sen's way out: allo (@draw sen_t@)w some comparisons between people, and sensible rules come back. The comparisons are the price of any answer.

    erase sen_t

B: So how sure are we which rule is right?
    highlight bar_1 bar_2 bar_3

Open. (@draw open@) It's a value judgement that no data can settle. What eco (@highlight open@)nomics can do is make the weights visible.

B: And some governments do write them down. Britain' (@draw uk@)s Treasury guidance counts a pound to a household with half the median income about two point four times as much.

    hide axes legend bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 uk open

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want the three rules as formulas?

@math

    erase mathq

Utilitarian: society's score W is everyone's U added up.
    draw m_box m_line_1

Rawls: W is the smallest U, and nothing else.
    draw m_line_2

And Anthony Atkinson's family runs between them: with epsilon zero it's the plain sum, and as epsilon grows it moves toward Rawls.
    draw m_line_3

    erase m

@test

    erase mathq

    show uk open

    erase axes legend bar_1 bar_2 bar_3

    quiz "Plan X gives the richer group 10 and the poorer 2. Plan Y gives 6 and 5. Which does a Rawlsian pick?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: Y: its worst-off group has 5, against 2 under X.
        wrong: Ignore the richer group and compare only the worst-off: here, Y.
        * X
        + Y
        * They tie

    quiz "A plan raises the richer group from 8 to 10 and leaves the poorer at 3. What does a strict Rawlsian rule say?" voice b
        right: No better, no worse: the worst-off still has 3, and that's the only number the rule reads.
        wrong: It reads only the worst-off's 3: here, no better, no worse.
        * Better
        + No better, no worse
        * Worse

    quiz "A utilitarian can give $100 to a millionaire or to a family scraping by. Each extra dollar matters less the more you have. Which adds more wellbeing?"
        right: The family: the same $100 buys more wellbeing where money is scarce, so even plain adding up favours them.
        wrong: The same $100 means far less to someone who already has a lot: here, the family.
        * The millionaire
        + The family
        * The same

    draw axes legend bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 parallel true

So how do we add up everyone's welfare? Only by deciding whose points count for how much. The honest part is saying which weights you chose.
    highlight open effect circle

    pause 0.5

## Where next
